WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:04.178
- We don't score a touchdown every time,

00:00:04.178 --> 00:00:05.219
but we move the ball forward.

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:06.958
John F. Kennedy didn't look up at the moon

00:00:06.958 --> 00:00:08.675
and say aww, that's too far.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.387
None of us can afford to be complacent.

00:00:11.387 --> 00:00:13.651
Stuff gets better if we work at it.

00:00:13.651 --> 00:00:17.138
It wasn't because of my
brilliance or something

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:18.668
that these things happened.

00:00:18.668 --> 00:00:21.526
They spend time thinking about polls

00:00:21.526 --> 00:00:22.378
but not about principle.

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:26.018
Nobody cares how tough
your upbringing was.

00:00:26.018 --> 00:00:27.587
You can't take yourself too seriously,

00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:28.501
you have to take the job seriously.

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:31.402
We get real exercise for about two weeks

00:00:31.402 --> 00:00:33.811
and then we fall asleep for two years.

00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:35.853
Winston Churchill was dismissed

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:38.202
as little more than a has-been

00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:40.730
who enjoyed scotch a little bit too much.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.922
- He's the 44th and current
president of the United States

00:00:43.922 --> 00:00:47.186
and the first African
American to hold the office.

00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:49.435
- He's a graduate of Columbia University

00:00:49.435 --> 00:00:50.333
and Harvard Law School.

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:54.442
- He was inaugurated as
president on January 20th, 2009.

00:00:54.442 --> 00:00:55.859
- He's Barack Obama

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:59.359
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.812
- We have looked at a
whole slew of problems

00:01:04.812 --> 00:01:06.788
when we came into office.

00:01:06.788 --> 00:01:10.759
And we've said, where can we
advance the ball down the field

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:13.926
each and every time, across the board.

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:20.101
And we don't score a touchdown every time,

00:01:20.101 --> 00:01:21.669
but we move the ball forward.

00:01:21.669 --> 00:01:23.071
- Do you feel like you've hit the metrics

00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:25.005
that you wanted to hit, even within--

00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:26.599
- You're always going to fall short

00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:29.205
because if you're hitting your marks

00:01:29.205 --> 00:01:31.021
that means you didn't
set them high enough.

00:01:31.021 --> 00:01:33.573
That's what hope is, imagining,

00:01:33.573 --> 00:01:36.383
and then fighting for,
and then working for

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:39.216
what did not seem possible before.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:42.221
That's leadership.

00:01:42.221 --> 00:01:43.982
John F. Kennedy didn't look up at the moon

00:01:43.982 --> 00:01:46.605
and say aww, that's too far.

00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:48.688
We can't go, false hopes.

00:01:51.125 --> 00:01:52.869
Martin Luther King
didn't stand on the steps

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:56.021
of the Lincoln Memorial and say,

00:01:56.021 --> 00:01:59.517
go home everybody, the
dream's deferred, false hopes.

00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:02.085
Ya'll need a reality check.

00:02:02.085 --> 00:02:05.085
(audience applauds)

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:10.045
There is a moment in the
life of every generation

00:02:10.045 --> 00:02:14.237
when that spirit of hopefulness
has to come through,

00:02:14.237 --> 00:02:16.541
if we are to make our mark on history.

00:02:16.541 --> 00:02:20.382
We too often let the
external, the material things

00:02:20.382 --> 00:02:23.589
serve as indicators that we're doing well,

00:02:23.589 --> 00:02:25.725
even though something inside us tells us

00:02:25.725 --> 00:02:28.152
that we're not doing our best.

00:02:28.152 --> 00:02:30.373
That we're avoiding that which is hard,

00:02:30.373 --> 00:02:33.206
but also necessary, that
we're shrinking from

00:02:33.206 --> 00:02:37.605
rather than rising to the
challenges of the age.

00:02:37.605 --> 00:02:41.245
And the thing is, in this
new hyper-competitive age,

00:02:41.245 --> 00:02:45.412
none of us, none of us can
afford to be complacent.

00:02:46.685 --> 00:02:50.268
That's true whatever
profession you choose.

00:02:51.229 --> 00:02:54.013
Professors might earn
the distinction of tenure

00:02:54.013 --> 00:02:55.829
but that doesn't guarantee that they'll

00:02:55.829 --> 00:02:58.512
keep putting in the long
hours and late nights

00:02:58.512 --> 00:03:02.679
and have the passion and the
drive to be great educators.

00:03:04.997 --> 00:03:07.349
The same principle is true
in your personal life.

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:11.213
Being a parent is not just a
matter of paying the bills,

00:03:11.213 --> 00:03:12.989
doing the bare minimum.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.311
It's not just bringing a child
into the world that matters,

00:03:16.311 --> 00:03:18.989
but the acts of love
and sacrifice it takes

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:21.765
to raise and educate that child,

00:03:21.765 --> 00:03:23.972
and give them opportunity.

00:03:23.972 --> 00:03:26.981
The one thing that I feel deeply about,

00:03:26.981 --> 00:03:28.997
and this is something
I'll feel deeply about

00:03:28.997 --> 00:03:31.247
when I leave government is,

00:03:33.712 --> 00:03:36.365
stuff gets better if we work at it

00:03:36.365 --> 00:03:39.501
and we stay focused on where we're going.

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:42.933
It doesn't immediately get all solved

00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:45.183
and I warned against this
when I was running for office,

00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:47.749
because everybody had the
hope posters and the this

00:03:47.749 --> 00:03:49.221
and the that and everybody
was feeling like--

00:03:49.221 --> 00:03:50.054
- Hey, we didn't make
those, you made those!

00:03:50.054 --> 00:03:50.887
(audience laughing)

00:03:50.887 --> 00:03:51.954
- No, no, no, no!
- What'dya blamin' us?

00:03:51.954 --> 00:03:53.353
- No, no, no, no, no!
- That wasn't us.

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:57.106
- Hang on a second.
No, no, no, I like them.

00:03:57.106 --> 00:03:58.956
- I'm pretty sure that came from you.

00:03:58.956 --> 00:04:00.339
- It was a nice poster.
- All right.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.275
- No, but what I'm saying is that,

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:04.129
if you look at what I said at the time,

00:04:04.129 --> 00:04:07.842
I said, "This is going to
be an ongoing project".

00:04:07.842 --> 00:04:09.823
And it's a project of citizens.

00:04:09.823 --> 00:04:11.656
It's not just, fix it.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.907
It's how do we work together

00:04:14.907 --> 00:04:16.490
to get things done,

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.522
and it will be imperfect.

00:04:19.522 --> 00:04:20.355
- [John] Yes.

00:04:20.355 --> 00:04:22.435
- But, over time, is it better?

00:04:22.435 --> 00:04:23.650
And here's the thing I can say, John.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:24.483
- [John] Yes.

00:04:24.483 --> 00:04:26.499
I can say this unequivocally.

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:29.962
The VA is better now than
when I came into office.

00:04:29.962 --> 00:04:32.155
It is better now than
when I came into office.

00:04:32.155 --> 00:04:35.130
Government works better than
when I came into office.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.275
The economy, by every metric, is better

00:04:38.275 --> 00:04:39.978
than when I came into office.

00:04:39.978 --> 00:04:41.815
And so, the reason I can sleep at night,

00:04:41.815 --> 00:04:42.659
(audience applauding)

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:46.705
is I say to myself, you
know what, it's better.

00:04:46.705 --> 00:04:48.045
Now, am I satisfied with it?

00:04:48.045 --> 00:04:50.077
No, and should voters
be satisfied with it?

00:04:50.077 --> 00:04:52.820
Absolutely not, because otherwise you know

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.372
if we get complacent and lazy
then stuff doesn't happen.

00:04:56.372 --> 00:04:58.930
You just don't succeed

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.940
in any endeavor

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.141
unless you've got a team
that's been supporting you.

00:05:04.141 --> 00:05:07.636
And, that's part of my
political philosophy.

00:05:07.636 --> 00:05:10.333
It's really based on my own experience,

00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:13.068
which was if somebody
hadn't been out there

00:05:13.068 --> 00:05:14.149
looking out for me,

00:05:14.149 --> 00:05:18.237
starting with my mom, my
grandmother, my grandfather,

00:05:18.237 --> 00:05:19.853
then I wouldn't have made it.

00:05:19.853 --> 00:05:23.620
It wasn't because of my
brilliance or something

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.328
that these things happened,
it had to do with people

00:05:26.328 --> 00:05:27.267
investing in ya.

00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:29.483
And so we've got to make
sure we're investing

00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:32.281
in the next generation, just
like somebody invested in us.

00:05:32.281 --> 00:05:33.812
I want to highlight two main problems

00:05:33.812 --> 00:05:37.729
with that old, tired
me-first approach to life.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:43.987
First of all, it distracts you
from what's truly important.

00:05:43.987 --> 00:05:46.611
And it may lead you to
compromise your values

00:05:46.611 --> 00:05:50.028
and your principles and your commitments.

00:05:51.403 --> 00:05:52.979
Think about it.

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:54.916
It's in chasing titles and status

00:05:54.916 --> 00:05:57.699
and worrying about the next election

00:05:57.699 --> 00:05:59.693
rather than the national interests

00:05:59.693 --> 00:06:03.963
and the interests of those who
you're supposed to represent

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:08.603
that politicians so often
lose their ways in Washington.

00:06:08.603 --> 00:06:09.542
(audience applauding)

00:06:09.542 --> 00:06:12.347
They spend time thinking about polls,

00:06:12.347 --> 00:06:15.412
but not about about principle.

00:06:15.412 --> 00:06:19.179
It was in pursuit of
gaudy short-term profits

00:06:19.179 --> 00:06:22.011
and the bonuses that came with them,

00:06:22.011 --> 00:06:26.635
that so many folks lost
their way on Wall Street,

00:06:26.635 --> 00:06:30.802
engaging in extraordinary risks
with other people's money.

00:06:33.779 --> 00:06:37.403
In contrast, the leaders we revere,

00:06:37.403 --> 00:06:40.903
the businesses and institutions that last,

00:06:41.883 --> 00:06:45.483
they are not generally the
result of a narrow pursuit

00:06:45.483 --> 00:06:48.732
of popularity or personal advancement

00:06:48.732 --> 00:06:52.571
but of devotion to some bigger purpose.

00:06:52.571 --> 00:06:55.913
The preservation of the
Union, or the determination

00:06:55.913 --> 00:06:59.667
to lift a country out of a depression.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:02.860
The creation of a quality product.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.910
A commitment to your
customers, your workers,

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.451
your shareholders, and your community.

00:07:08.451 --> 00:07:11.099
A commitment to make
sure that an institution

00:07:11.099 --> 00:07:14.435
like ASU is inclusive and diverse

00:07:14.435 --> 00:07:16.083
and giving opportunity to all.

00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:17.666
That's the hallmark

00:07:19.341 --> 00:07:21.236
of real success.

00:07:21.236 --> 00:07:22.971
We know that too many
young men in our community

00:07:22.971 --> 00:07:25.756
continue to make bad choices.

00:07:25.756 --> 00:07:30.625
And I have to say growing up,
I made quite a few myself.

00:07:30.625 --> 00:07:33.427
Sometimes I wrote off my own failings

00:07:33.427 --> 00:07:35.853
as just another example
of the world trying

00:07:35.853 --> 00:07:38.651
to keep a Black man down.

00:07:38.651 --> 00:07:42.234
I had a tendency
sometimes, to make excuses

00:07:43.068 --> 00:07:46.459
for me not doing the right thing.

00:07:46.459 --> 00:07:48.492
But one of the things that
all of you have learned

00:07:48.492 --> 00:07:51.156
over the last four years
is there's no longer

00:07:51.156 --> 00:07:53.611
any room for excuses.

00:07:53.611 --> 00:07:56.778
(audience applauding)

00:07:58.251 --> 00:08:00.867
I understand there's a
common Fraternity creed

00:08:00.867 --> 00:08:05.427
here at Morehouse, "Excuses
are tools of the incompetent

00:08:05.427 --> 00:08:07.459
"used to build bridges to nowhere

00:08:07.459 --> 00:08:10.042
"and monuments of nothingness."

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:14.707
Well, we've got no time for excuses.

00:08:14.707 --> 00:08:16.683
Not because the bitter legacy of slavery

00:08:16.683 --> 00:08:21.635
and segregation have vanished
entirely, they have not.

00:08:21.635 --> 00:08:23.412
Not because racism and discrimination

00:08:23.412 --> 00:08:27.579
no longer exist, we know
those are still out there.

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:31.675
It's just that in today's hyper-connected,

00:08:31.675 --> 00:08:34.703
hyper-competitive world with
millions of young people

00:08:34.703 --> 00:08:37.651
from China and India and Brazil,

00:08:37.651 --> 00:08:39.566
many of whom started with a whole lot less

00:08:39.566 --> 00:08:41.707
than all of you did,

00:08:41.707 --> 00:08:45.843
all of them entering the
global workforce alongside you,

00:08:45.843 --> 00:08:48.225
nobody is going to give you anything

00:08:48.225 --> 00:08:50.308
that you have not earned.

00:08:51.475 --> 00:08:54.843
Nobody cares how tough
your upbringing was.

00:08:54.843 --> 00:08:58.926
Nobody cares if you suffered
some discrimination.

00:09:01.427 --> 00:09:04.395
And moreover, you have
to remember that whatever

00:09:04.395 --> 00:09:07.323
you've gone through,
it pales in comparison

00:09:07.323 --> 00:09:10.166
to the hardships previous
generations endured

00:09:10.166 --> 00:09:11.651
and they overcame them.

00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:14.803
And if they overcame them,
you can overcome them too.

00:09:14.803 --> 00:09:16.436
Part of the way that
you survive the stress

00:09:16.436 --> 00:09:17.918
of the White House

00:09:17.918 --> 00:09:19.588
is being able to laugh with your team

00:09:19.588 --> 00:09:23.452
about some of the crazy
stuff that happens.

00:09:23.452 --> 00:09:26.001
And, you know, you can't
take yourself too seriously,

00:09:26.001 --> 00:09:27.697
you have to take the job seriously.

00:09:27.697 --> 00:09:29.497
You have to take your
responsibilities seriously.

00:09:29.497 --> 00:09:31.501
But, you have to be able
to laugh at yourself

00:09:31.501 --> 00:09:32.812
first and foremost in order

00:09:32.812 --> 00:09:35.053
to be able to manage the whole thing.

00:09:35.053 --> 00:09:37.085
You have to have a plan,

00:09:37.085 --> 00:09:39.335
you have to have a strategy

00:09:40.837 --> 00:09:42.837
and then you have to have stick-to-it-ness

00:09:42.837 --> 00:09:44.309
because you know the strategy

00:09:44.309 --> 00:09:47.559
is not going to immediately bear fruit.

00:09:49.637 --> 00:09:52.149
The American character is one that lurches

00:09:52.149 --> 00:09:54.232
between spasm and trance.

00:09:59.453 --> 00:10:02.117
We get real exercise for about two weeks

00:10:02.117 --> 00:10:05.284
and then we fall asleep for two years.

00:10:06.653 --> 00:10:09.917
And what Charles Hamilton
Houston understood is

00:10:09.917 --> 00:10:12.584
that our vision extends decades.

00:10:14.733 --> 00:10:16.650
It extends generations.

00:10:17.701 --> 00:10:20.589
And those young men I talked about,

00:10:20.589 --> 00:10:23.989
we may not be able to
reach them right now,

00:10:23.989 --> 00:10:25.109
but I tell you what,

00:10:25.109 --> 00:10:29.525
if we set the trend lines
just a little bit better

00:10:29.525 --> 00:10:32.858
then the infant that's crying in Compton

00:10:34.541 --> 00:10:38.124
or Harlem, or Anacostia,
or the ninth ward,

00:10:40.023 --> 00:10:43.767
that infant may have a different future.

00:10:43.767 --> 00:10:45.759
And then when that infant
has a different future,

00:10:45.759 --> 00:10:47.679
the country has a different future.

00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:49.935
And then we as a people
have a different future.

00:10:49.935 --> 00:10:53.685
So, there's got to be
an understanding of how

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.893
time can actually help us move mountains

00:11:00.415 --> 00:11:04.582
if we're working with time,
we're not waiting for time,

00:11:05.983 --> 00:11:07.351
we're working with it.

00:11:07.351 --> 00:11:09.592
You may have setbacks
and you may have failures

00:11:09.592 --> 00:11:10.895
but you're not done.

00:11:10.895 --> 00:11:12.799
You're not even getting started.

00:11:12.799 --> 00:11:14.382
Not by a long shot.

00:11:15.691 --> 00:11:18.063
And if you ever forget
that, just look to history.

00:11:18.063 --> 00:11:22.895
Thomas Paine was a failed
corset maker, a failed teacher

00:11:22.895 --> 00:11:25.583
and a failed tax collector

00:11:25.583 --> 00:11:27.215
before he made his mark on history

00:11:27.215 --> 00:11:29.583
with a little book called, Common Sense

00:11:29.583 --> 00:11:32.091
that helped ignite a revolution.

00:11:32.091 --> 00:11:35.258
(audience applauding)

00:11:37.647 --> 00:11:39.727
Julia Child didn't
publish her first cookbook

00:11:39.727 --> 00:11:41.799
until she was almost 50.

00:11:41.799 --> 00:11:43.279
Colonel Sanders didn't open up his first

00:11:43.279 --> 00:11:45.511
Kentucky Fried Chicken
until he was in his 60s.

00:11:45.511 --> 00:11:46.927
(audience laughing)

00:11:46.927 --> 00:11:49.847
Winston Churchill was
dismissed as little more

00:11:49.847 --> 00:11:53.825
than a has-been who enjoyed
scotch a little bit too much

00:11:53.825 --> 00:11:55.534
before he took over as Prime Minister

00:11:55.534 --> 00:11:59.099
and saw Great Britain
through it's finest hour.

00:11:59.099 --> 00:12:01.230
No one thought a former football player

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:04.052
stocking shelves at the local supermarket

00:12:04.052 --> 00:12:05.675
would return to the game he loved

00:12:05.675 --> 00:12:07.483
to become a Super Bowl MVP

00:12:07.483 --> 00:12:09.100
and then come here to Arizona

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:12.075
and lead your Cardinals
to their first Super Bowl.

00:12:12.075 --> 00:12:13.787
(audience applauding)

00:12:13.787 --> 00:12:16.454
Your body of work is never done.

00:12:19.723 --> 00:12:22.531
Each of them at one point in their life

00:12:22.531 --> 00:12:26.971
didn't have any title, or
much status to speak of.

00:12:26.971 --> 00:12:28.781
But they had passion.

00:12:28.781 --> 00:12:30.417
A commitment to following that passion

00:12:30.417 --> 00:12:32.035
wherever it would lead

00:12:32.035 --> 00:12:35.524
and to working hard
every step along the way.

00:12:35.524 --> 00:12:36.675
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:36.675 --> 00:12:40.165
We made this video because
Jerome A. asked us to.

00:12:40.165 --> 00:12:41.467
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:12:41.467 --> 00:12:43.299
that you want me to profile next,

00:12:43.299 --> 00:12:44.883
leave it in the comments below

00:12:44.883 --> 00:12:46.291
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:46.291 --> 00:12:49.515
- And please let us know
which of the top 10 rules

00:12:49.515 --> 00:12:52.171
that you like the most,
please leave a comment below

00:12:52.171 --> 00:12:53.835
and we will join in the discussion.

00:12:53.835 --> 00:12:54.923
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:54.923 --> 00:12:57.219
Continue to believe,
and we'll see you soon.

00:12:57.219 --> 00:12:58.052
- Bye.

